WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.970
- Getting comfortable with
being uncomfortable for a period

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.522
of time, is actually a great life skill.

00:00:06.522 --> 00:00:09.150
(motivational bass music)

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.643
You got your perspective.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.204
(crowd cheering)

00:00:14.204 --> 00:00:15.037
I just wanna be happy.

00:00:15.037 --> 00:00:16.070
Don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:18.993 --> 00:00:21.657
- Elia, I'm so happy for you to meet Gary.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.559
- Thank you, I'm really happy as well.

00:00:25.559 --> 00:00:26.583
- (indistinct) before we started?

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.455
- So I was studying, Maha,
all about how I relate to you

00:00:32.455 --> 00:00:35.520
in the way that you used to
think about school a lot.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.230
And I really feel like the same.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:43.100
And I have that entrepreneurial
mindset that you have.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.810
And I always...

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.897
I follow you on TikToK and Instagram,

00:00:46.897 --> 00:00:49.220
and I watch videos every day.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:52.150
And every time I hear
you talk about something

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.405
that I can relate to, I
completely relate to it.

00:00:56.405 --> 00:01:00.180
And me and my parents always
have this debate about school.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.344
And I tell them like, I
want to start a business.

00:01:03.344 --> 00:01:04.300
I want to get into drop shipping.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.133
I want to do this.

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:07.305
And then my mother's
like, "No do chemistry

00:01:07.305 --> 00:01:08.920
and then all of that."

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:12.400
So when I heard that my
dad was working with you,

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.650
I was like, "Dad, I
watch Gary V every day.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:18.027
He's one of my idols, like
really," and then he was like,

00:01:18.027 --> 00:01:20.120
"I'll try my best to
get them to meet you."

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.350
And when he told me that that
he organized the meeting,

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:25.710
it was like this is like the best thing

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:26.700
I could ever ask for.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.360
And so I want to really thank you so much

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.670
for this amazing opportunity.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.164
- Of course Elia, you can't imagine

00:01:33.164 --> 00:01:33.997
how that feels to be heard.

00:01:33.997 --> 00:01:35.860
So thank you so much for those kind words.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.213
Is there anything you want to
focus on on this conversation?

00:01:40.114 --> 00:01:45.114
- I mean I wanna get
your perspective on what

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.514
I should do your advice
on how I should act.

00:01:48.514 --> 00:01:51.990
Because like, obviously there's the side

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.400
of my parents telling me one thing,

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.875
and the side of what I really want to do.

00:01:56.875 --> 00:02:00.240
And then, there is also
this like mental place.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.360
That it's like my parents are providing me

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.020
with everything and
they're really amazing.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.920
And I love them so much.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.030
And they give me great advice,

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.210
but on this specific thing,

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:14.692
I really feel like I want
to try and explore my

00:02:14.692 --> 00:02:19.633
strengths and my weaknesses
and learn from my mistakes.

00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:22.500
If I start a business or drop shipping

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.290
or whatever it is, early.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.790
And I just, I can't get
that across to my parents.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.077
- I think a lot of times
people in life think about

00:02:32.077 --> 00:02:35.157
"or", when the answer is actually "and".

00:02:37.728 --> 00:02:39.970
I wasn't able to convince my parents

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:42.053
either to not go to college.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.990
You know, I didn't want to,

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:45.823
- [Elia] Yeah.

00:02:45.823 --> 00:02:46.910
- And I went for four years

00:02:47.978 --> 00:02:48.950
because I loved my parents so much.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:51.760
And I just, I think...

00:02:52.957 --> 00:02:54.245
I'm sure Maha can tell you the same thing

00:02:54.245 --> 00:02:55.790
and anybody who's lived a little bit.

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.360
Getting comfortable with being
uncomfortable for a period

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.092
of time is actually a great life skill.

00:03:01.092 --> 00:03:04.170
And honestly, no matter how much,

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.953
including myself, one
loves entrepreneurship,

00:03:06.953 --> 00:03:09.820
the vacation

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:11.710
that is being young

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:15.500
and just going to school
is, that's what I...

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.420
The reason I was able
to be okay the last four

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.700
or five years mentally
was, I basically said, well

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.678
this will be my last real vacation

00:03:24.678 --> 00:03:27.653
before I go into my real life.

00:03:28.755 --> 00:03:32.240
And so, I think what I was
fortunate enough was my parents

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.100
didn't stop me from doing
business stuff on the side

00:03:38.152 --> 00:03:39.910
because obviously I worked

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:41.800
for my family's liquor store business

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.580
and I was doing my sports card

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:45.980
and flipping and garage sailing.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:47.843
And so-
- [Elia] Yeah.

00:03:48.691 --> 00:03:50.581
- If you're in a position
where your parents

00:03:50.581 --> 00:03:53.210
aren't completely stopping you
from doing things on the side

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:55.964
and saying, no you
can't, you're forbidden.

00:03:55.964 --> 00:03:58.060
You must only study.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:01.550
If you're lucky enough
that you can do both,

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.830
I would tell you that you've plenty

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:06.185
of time in front of you,

00:04:06.185 --> 00:04:07.392
- [Elia] Yeah.

00:04:07.392 --> 00:04:08.866
- To do what you want.

00:04:08.866 --> 00:04:11.620
But I do think the sooner you
can start tasting, you know,

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:16.620
drop shipping, influencer
marketing, NFT, cryptocurrency.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.509
All this stuff looks easy on paper.

00:04:19.509 --> 00:04:21.230
It's good in theory.

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:24.210
But then when you do it, it's
much harder than people think.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:29.210
And I think the sooner you can
taste the ups and the downs

00:04:30.195 --> 00:04:32.310
it's kind of like exercising.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:35.267
You'll be more prepared for
when you do it full time.

00:04:35.267 --> 00:04:37.421
It's like prepping for a fight.

00:04:37.421 --> 00:04:38.254
- [Elia] Yeah.

00:04:38.254 --> 00:04:39.300
- [Gary] You'll be ready to fight.

00:04:40.292 --> 00:04:43.040
So I think, are you in a
position where you can start

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:45.640
doing some of this stuff on the side?

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:46.520
- [Elia] I think I can.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:50.133
What I spoke to my dad about
was doing this in summer.

00:04:52.619 --> 00:04:57.313
And my dad had a meeting
with an American businessman,

00:04:59.805 --> 00:05:01.580
and he's based in Arizona.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:05.040
And he has a lot of friends
in real estate and everything.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.040
And he came to our house a week
ago, and I have plans to go

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:13.930
and visit him in Arizona
and expand my knowledge

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:15.830
about, because I'm really
interested in real estate

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:17.900
as well, doing real estate in the future.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:21.303
So I basically planned my
whole summer to do that.

00:05:23.552 --> 00:05:27.197
And what my dad tells me a lot is that,

00:05:27.197 --> 00:05:28.510
he says that I'm lazy.

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:32.730
And I don't disagree with him, I can be.

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:36.130
I have the tendency to
be lazy, but I also...

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:38.900
- You're lazy 'cause you're disinterested.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.343
- Exactly! Exactly 100%.

00:05:41.343 --> 00:05:43.390
- When I was 13 to 18,

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:46.790
I was told by many, many
people that I was lazy.

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:51.350
They just happened to be
people that were at school.

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:52.183
- Yeah.

00:05:53.476 --> 00:05:55.050
- And maybe my grandma sometimes

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.060
around the house, because I didn't want to

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:58.113
hang a picture,

00:06:02.094 --> 00:06:02.927
but it makes me laugh so much

00:06:02.927 --> 00:06:05.820
because people thought I was lazy,

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:09.563
yet my life's destiny was to
be synonymous with work ethic.

00:06:12.195 --> 00:06:16.710
- Yeah, I really feel like if
I had something interesting,

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:19.565
when I watch all those
videos about business

00:06:19.565 --> 00:06:24.039
and marketing and real estate,
I spend like three hours,

00:06:24.039 --> 00:06:26.508
just watching and just listening.

00:06:26.508 --> 00:06:28.020
And it's so interesting.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.070
And I'm so captivated in the moment.

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:33.010
But as soon as I switch
back to my chemistry class

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:35.370
and they started talking about
all this other stuff, like

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:37.946
in two minutes, I'm so disinterested.

00:06:37.946 --> 00:06:40.970
So uninterested in whatever
they're talking about.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:43.513
- I lived your life my
friend, I understand.

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:47.323
It got so bad for me
that I started bringing

00:06:47.323 --> 00:06:50.230
wine magazines to school

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:53.009
and I would read them
in the middle of class.

00:06:53.009 --> 00:06:54.203
Like literally.
- Wow.

00:06:55.582 --> 00:06:57.770
- I basically realized something.

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:01.100
I realized that my high
school was more interested

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:02.830
in me passing because they were going

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:05.295
for a blue ribbon status in New Jersey,

00:07:05.295 --> 00:07:07.541
which required everybody graduating.

00:07:07.541 --> 00:07:10.040
I realized that I could,

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:15.040
as long as I showed up for
school, that I was going to pass.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.740
And the second I realized that,

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:22.620
I basically read wine magazines in class

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:27.513
or fooled around or daydreamed
or doodled or whatever.

00:07:29.709 --> 00:07:32.133
I think the key here is perspective.

00:07:34.247 --> 00:07:36.660
One, you're not missing out.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:40.190
Today it's NFTs, tomorrow it's VR.

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:41.700
The next day, it's something that

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.610
not the three of us could even imagine.

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:44.842
- Yeah.

00:07:44.842 --> 00:07:45.675
- So I think we've got to make sure

00:07:45.675 --> 00:07:48.224
that you don't have
anxiety of missing out.

00:07:48.224 --> 00:07:51.110
Once you have a good
relationship with that,

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:53.740
what you need to do is just
focus on learning as much

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:57.990
as you can, around the things
that you're passionate about.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:02.990
Ultimately, your parents
will be okay no matter what,

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:05.220
as long as you're healthy and happy,

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:06.710
but you might have to have a little bit

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:09.800
of pain in between now and that success.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:14.800
- Yeah. I mean one story about
that you were talking about,

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:19.930
that really sort of got
me really interested was,

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:22.040
I remember when you said that it was,

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:24.090
it was I think around Christmas time

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:26.220
and you were in the
working in the liquor store

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.450
and you were making
crazy amounts and hour.

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:30.716
And this lady that lived
like two hours away,

00:08:30.716 --> 00:08:34.274
she ordered this pink
rose bottle or something.

00:08:34.274 --> 00:08:36.480
And then you had to get in your car

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:38.860
and drive all the way in the snow.

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:41.890
And then I felt like I was...

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:45.300
When I watched that video,
I literally I said out loud,

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.420
like, this is so genius.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:48.820
This is so what I want to be doing.

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:51.209
I wanna have my business.

00:08:51.209 --> 00:08:55.620
I want every customer to
know that whatever they order

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:58.169
is going to be there, that's quality.

00:08:58.169 --> 00:09:00.200
That's what I study.

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:03.850
And I'm in a better position
than maybe when you were

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:05.853
when you were in school.

00:09:05.853 --> 00:09:08.870
Next year, I only do three
subjects that I like,

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:11.583
so I'm going to do business,
economics and politics.

00:09:11.583 --> 00:09:15.619
So I told my dad that next year,

00:09:15.619 --> 00:09:18.940
I can get like everything that,

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:22.320
all the grades that I
would need and everything

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:23.530
because it would be interesting.

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:26.159
And I told my dad, like, I'm
going to go to university.

00:09:26.159 --> 00:09:30.080
Maybe not, it's like more of a safety net.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:34.340
So if something happens, I have a plan B.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:36.508
I have a degree to fall
back on something that

00:09:36.508 --> 00:09:40.421
people look for when they're hiring.

00:09:40.421 --> 00:09:43.000
- By the time you're hiring in six...

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.430
Let's say you go through that

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:46.230
and it's university four years for you.

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:47.493
Is that how it works? Or three? Or four?

00:09:47.493 --> 00:09:49.450
- Yeah four years, yeah.

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:53.580
- By the time that'll
be five years of school,

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:54.970
it sounds like, 'cause
you have one more year.

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:57.170
Is that what you said
Elia? And then university?

00:09:57.170 --> 00:09:58.480
- I have two more years.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:00.790
- Two more years, I mean, that's six.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:02.360
Then you try to do your thing.

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:04.460
Let's say it fails after two years.

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:07.840
In eight years, the amount of businesses

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:10.166
that are going to be hiring
without university is enormous.

00:10:10.166 --> 00:10:12.160
I would tell you to think

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:15.160
about it differently,
and perspective matters.

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:18.770
Instead of thinking about
it as your fallback plan,

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:21.950
because the reality is,
if you keep networking,

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:25.340
I mean if you just stay in
touch with Maha, just her!

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:27.620
Just her, for the next eight years,

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:30.480
every six months, 'hello' texts, whatever,

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:32.960
she'll be more likely
to help you get a job

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:34.780
that's meaningful for you,

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:37.171
then your university.

00:10:37.171 --> 00:10:38.470
I promise you getting
a job with me is more

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:40.293
about your relationship
with Maha than some piece

00:10:40.293 --> 00:10:43.585
of paper of some school that
I've never heard of. Promise!

00:10:43.585 --> 00:10:45.030
(Maha giggling)

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:46.550
- I think the key is perspective.

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:49.300
I think if you think about university

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:53.800
as something that you want
to do for your parents

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:56.070
because they've done so much for you,

00:10:56.070 --> 00:11:00.580
you'd be surprised on how it
feels going through university.

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:04.158
I believe that if you think,
I believe that in your heart

00:11:04.158 --> 00:11:08.340
that it being a fallback
piece of paper is bullshit.

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:10.550
But I don't believe that you think going

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:12.260
for four years for your
parents is bullshit.

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:15.030
As a matter of fact, based
on the way you're talking,

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:16.610
I think that it would make you happy

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:18.440
to do that for your parents.

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:19.670
- It would make me happy.

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:24.620
And I know that because
the thing is that my dad,

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:27.370
he went through the normal process

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:28.920
and it was really hard for him,

00:11:31.649 --> 00:11:34.915
and he had to work so hard.

00:11:34.915 --> 00:11:36.990
And it was such a disadvantage
because of his circumstances.

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:39.420
There was Civil War and
everything when he was growing up.

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:44.420
So for him, the fact that he
made success as an employee

00:11:44.481 --> 00:11:49.290
and as like working for a
company after so many years

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:50.990
and the growth of the company with,

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:53.706
while he was with them,
exponentially grew.

00:11:53.706 --> 00:11:58.706
So that's why I always
completely understand my dad

00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:01.460
when he tells me like, you
have to go to university,

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:05.303
this is the only way, like,
what else are you going to do?

00:12:06.141 --> 00:12:09.230
No job is going to look
for you without a degree.

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:12.246
And then I always tell my dad like, dad,

00:12:12.246 --> 00:12:17.180
take a quote from Elon Musk,
"The people that I didn't go to

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:19.580
Harvard, but the people
that worked for me did."

00:12:20.449 --> 00:12:22.386
And then my mother was
like, and then my dad always

00:12:22.386 --> 00:12:25.262
tells me how many Elon Musks
are there in the world?

00:12:25.262 --> 00:12:26.095
And I told him-

00:12:26.095 --> 00:12:27.950
- The problem is there's a lot more.

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:31.022
They're not Elon Musk,
but the amount of people

00:12:31.022 --> 00:12:36.022
that are entrepreneurs that
were not built for school,

00:12:36.051 --> 00:12:39.750
that have gone on to successful,
but more importantly,

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:44.750
happy lives that have far more
college educated students.

00:12:45.042 --> 00:12:50.042
Literally almost everybody
that works for my companies,

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:52.650
the 1500 current employees and the tens

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:55.700
of thousands that have
worked for me in my life.

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:58.370
Literally almost all of
them outside of a couple

00:12:58.370 --> 00:13:02.433
scrappers at wine library,
stock boys, have been on paper

00:13:02.433 --> 00:13:04.820
more educated than me.

00:13:04.820 --> 00:13:08.130
Forget about my financial
success in comparison

00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:10.040
to the majority of them.

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:13.766
